// Path validation of the pure TLS client (#3303, #1298).
//
// The pure client accepted any path whose names matched and whose signatures
// verified: it never asked whether an issuer was a CA at all. A certificate a
// CA issued to any web site (cA FALSE) could sign a certificate for any other
// host, and the client trusted it. The first cases below are that shape,
// through chain_verify_path, the call the client made; each failed before
// the fix (the forged certificate was accepted) and is refused now. The rest
// cover what validate_chain checks on the way: keyUsage keyCertSign,
// pathLenConstraint, extendedKeyUsage for the purpose, name constraints, an
// unknown critical extension, and validity at a chosen time; then what the
// x509-limbo corpus showed missing (#1298): weak and explicit-curve keys, a
// wildcard under an excluded subtree, the max_depth bound, a leaf whose
// keyUsage does not allow signing (#3309), and trust anchors that are not
// self-signed roots (#3310). The signature algorithms it showed missing are
// crypto_tls13_sig_algs (#3307).
//
// The fixtures are a PKI made once with openssl (`req -new`, `x509 -req
// -extfile`, valid 2026-01-01 to 2126-01-01), ECDSA P-256 unless named:
//   root        self-signed CA (basicConstraints cA, keyUsage keyCertSign)
//   ica         CA under root;  leaf (good.example, serverAuth) under ica
//   ee          an end entity of root (cA FALSE, ee.example)
//   forged      victim.example, signed with ee's key
//   eenobc      an end entity of root with no basicConstraints at all
//   forged2     victim.example, signed with eenobc's key
//   clientonly  extendedKeyUsage clientAuth only, under ica
//   ica0        CA under root with pathLenConstraint 0; subca under ica0;
//               subleaf under subca
//   icanoks     CA under root whose keyUsage lacks keyCertSign; nksleaf under it
//   icanc       CA under root, name constraints permit good.example only;
//               ncin (in.good.example) and ncout (other.example) under it
//   crit        under ica, with an unknown critical extension
//   icabadku    CA under root whose keyUsage extnValue is an OCTET STRING;
//               badkuleaf under it
//   icadup      CA under root with keyUsage twice (cRLSign, then keyCertSign:
//               the second was made as 2.5.29.16 and its OID patched, the
//               certificate signed again); dupleaf under it
//   icabool     CA under root whose basicConstraints cA is the BOOLEAN 0x01;
//               boolleaf under it
//   weak        a 1024-bit RSA leaf under ica
//   explicit    a P-256 leaf under ica whose key gives the curve explicitly
//   icaex       CA under root excluding bar.example.com; wild (*.example.com)
//               and wildok (*.other.com) under it
//   kunoca      under root, keyUsage keyCertSign and no basicConstraints;
//               kunocaleaf under it
//   akikeyid, akiissuer, akiserial, akiall
//               self-issued roots whose authorityKeyIdentifier names another
//               key, issuer (CN=Someone Else), serial, or all three, each
//               with a leaf (akikeyidleaf and so on); akiself, whose AKI
//               names its own serial, and akiselfleaf
//   pinned      a self-signed leaf (peer.example, cA FALSE), its own anchor
//   rootpl0     a self-signed root with pathLenConstraint 0 (#3432); icapl,
//               a CA under it, and plleaf (pl.example) under icapl; pldirect
//               (direct.example), an end entity of rootpl0. OpenSSL refuses
//               plleaf ("path length constraint exceeded") and takes pldirect.

import std.list
import std.string
import std.cryptography.tls13_cert

extern exit(code: int)

